preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: >-
    Semantic Fragmentation and Stochastic Assembly, version 2: A Protocol for
    Decentralized Language-Model Inference over Untrusted Volunteer Nodes
  abstract: >-
    Whitepaper version 2 of the Swarmbly protocol. It supersedes v1.4 and adds a
    theory of the fragment, five falsifiable models measured over 473 real runs,
    and a direct measurement of extraction by small nodes with aggregation by
    code. Version 1.4 specified the Swarmbly protocol: semantic decomposition of
    a request into micro-tasks, asynchronous dispatch to volunteer nodes each
    running a complete small language model, and local reassembly by a
    client-side orchestrator. Its three substantive contributions are the
    context budget S as the single scalar governing coherence, verifiability,
    privacy and required worker capability; a coverage model for semantic
    assembly giving the design equation c >= ln(1/eps)/(1-p); and consensus by
    multiple alignment of cross-family replicas, which returns a per-unit
    agreement score and a map of low-confidence regions that centralized
    inference cannot produce. Limitations and negative results are stated in
    full.
  authors:
    - given-names: "Sebastián A."
      family-names: "Espinoza-Ulloa"
      affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
      email: "sebas_saeu@hotmail.com"
      orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1497-356X"
  year: 2026
  month: 9
  # Paper DOI: the Zenodo record of version 2. The v1.4 record
  # (10.5281/zenodo.21957088) is the previous version of the same work. An arXiv
  # DOI will be added alongside it if and when the submission clears endorsement.
  doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23031305"
  url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23031305"
  # journal: not applicable while it is a preprint
  notes: >-
    Version 2, deposited on Zenodo 2026-09-29 (10.5281/zenodo.23031305);
    supersedes v1.4 (10.5281/zenodo.21957088, deposited 2026-08-14). Intended additional venue: arXiv,
    primary category cs.DC — deferred, because a first-time submitter to that
    archive requires endorsement and none had been obtained at time of
    publication. The prior-art position does not depend on arXiv: the Zenodo
    DOIs and the public repository are independent dated records.
